---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import TrackTag from "../components/TrackTag.astro";
import { TRACKS } from "../lib/taxonomy";
import { loadSources } from "../lib/sources";
const sources = loadSources();
---

<Base title="About">
  <main class="container about-page">
    <header class="about-header observation-grid">
      <p class="utility-label">Method & boundaries</p>
      <div><h1>A readable record of the AI × web negotiation.</h1><p>Crawler Policy turns changes in machine access, content rights, agent identity, governance, and web economics into concise English analysis — while keeping every conclusion tied to replayable evidence.</p></div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="section-index"><span class="utility-label">Editorial flow</span><strong>Evidence → Feed → Development → Weekly</strong></div>
      <div class="process">
        <article><span>Evidence</span><h2>Preserve what was observed</h2><p>Every successful fetch can create an immutable evidence record with source identity, timestamps, content hashes, and raw or normalized material. Failed analysis does not discard it.</p></article>
        <article><span>Feed</span><h2>Explain what happened</h2><p>Gemini produces a structured Summary, Insight, Implication, and Why it matters. Deterministic source rules label the result Verified, Reported, or Signal.</p></article>
        <article><span>Trend</span><h2>Change status with authority</h2><p>A durable trend can move only when primary or measurement-tier evidence supports the delta. No-change is a real result.</p></article>
        <article><span>Weekly</span><h2>Synthesize a completed window</h2><p>The weekly issue compares a full ISO week, shows quiet tracks, and refuses to publish when required health coverage is critical or stale.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="taxonomy-section">
      <div class="section-index"><span class="utility-label">Coverage</span><strong>{sources.length} configured sources · nine tracks</strong></div>
      <div><h2>Stable questions, explicit taxonomy</h2><p>Sources may cover multiple tracks. Nine internal tracks roll up into five public fronts so the site stays navigable without erasing technical distinctions.</p><div class="tracks">{TRACKS.map((track) => <TrackTag track={track} linked />)}</div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="rules">
      <div class="section-index"><span class="utility-label">Trust model</span><strong>What the labels mean</strong></div>
      <div class="rule-grid">
        <article><h2>Pipeline health is stage-aware</h2><p><strong>Healthy</strong> means all configured sources completed. <strong>Degraded</strong> means useful output exists but some non-required work failed. <strong>Critical</strong> means required evidence is incomplete, so weekly conclusions are withheld. <strong>Unknown</strong> is never promoted to healthy.</p></article>
        <article><h2>Gemini assists; evidence governs</h2><p>Gemini helps classify relevance, identify material change, and write the four analytical layers. It cannot promote a reported story into verified evidence. Source records, evidence IDs, and raw diffs remain authoritative.</p></article>
        <article><h2>Publication labels have consequences</h2><p><strong>Verified</strong> items use primary or measurement evidence. <strong>Reported</strong> items come from configured specialist reporting. <strong>Signals</strong> are early observations. Only verified material items can move trends or weekly conclusions.</p></article>
        <article><h2>Three dates stay separate</h2><p><code>event_date</code> is when the development occurred; <code>published_at</code> is when the source published it; <code>detected_at</code> is when this pipeline observed it.</p></article>
        <article><h2>Legacy is not current intelligence</h2><p>Schema-v1 events and the former Weekly Dispatch are preserved unchanged in <a href="/archive">Legacy Archive</a>. They remain useful context but never seed current trends or weekly issues.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-page { padding-top: var(--space-8); }
  .about-header { padding-bottom: var(--space-9); }
  .about-header > p { grid-column: 1 / 4; }
  .about-header > div { grid-column: 4 / 12; }
  .about-header h1 { max-width: 11ch; }
  .about-header div p { max-width: 65ch; color: var(--ink-muted); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  section { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 3fr; gap: var(--space-7); padding: var(--space-8) 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .section-index { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); color: var(--ink-muted); font: 0.7rem/1.5 var(--font-mono); }
  .process { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); }
  article { padding-top: var(--space-3); border-top: 3px solid var(--protocol-blue); }
  article > span { color: var(--protocol-blue); font: 650 0.65rem var(--font-mono); text-transform: uppercase; }
  article h2, .taxonomy-section h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; margin: var(--space-3) 0; }
  article p, .taxonomy-section p { color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 0.84rem; }
  .tracks { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .rule-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); }
  .rule-grid article { border-top-color: var(--border-strong); }
  code { color: var(--trace-violet); }
  @media (max-width: 800px) { .about-header, section, .process, .rule-grid { display: block; } .about-header > p, .section-index { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); } article { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); } }
</style>
